een the coronaviruses  uh so some of it is just that what uh  there's two two reasons one is or at  least two reasons there's probably many  but at least two of the driving ones are  that the virus itself  you know is shaped differently so uh the  spike protein is one we've all heard a  lot about  but the other proteins and components of  the virus the way it enters the cells is  different the shape of the nucleocapsid  uh the full varion capsule is different  in different coronal viruses so  that slight differentiation leads to  greater or less mobility and speed  as how it goes through cells and the  second really big reason  is the tropism of the virus changes in  two ways one is  where does the virus go in your body  which is the tropism in general what's  getting infected at what speed how long  does it persist  as i was saying earlier the lungs seem  to get infected early and then clear the  infection  even in cases where you know patients  ended up dying because we had the  autopsy  you know the virus was already gone from  the lungs but it had caused so much  damage and so much immune infiltration  that's what i ended up killing the  patients um and so you know it's a  tropism where the virus goes and then  what the virus what the virus does after  it's gone so what the tropism of  it's kind of like the shadow tropism of  how how many cells came in afterward  to disrupt what was present for the  normal biology  thank you so much and again i want to  thank our audience members for these  great questions  we have time for one more question dr  mason clearly the federal government has  made some mistakes  we can't turn back time but if you were  the president  right now why what would you do in  regards to mandates and protocols  to reduce the illnesses and deaths  moving forward  uh yeah great it is um it's always been  true you can't turn back the clock so  with the where we are today is what we  have to act on but if i  were president tomorrow and i ran as a